# Environment files

Treat every `.env` file as user-managed local configuration. This includes the repository root `.env` and any nested `.env` (for example `frontend/.env`).

## Do not change `.env`

Never create, edit, overwrite, rename, delete, or auto-format a `.env` file.

Reading `.env` is allowed only when needed to understand existing local configuration. Do not write it back.

`.env.example` and other `*.env.example` files are safe to edit.

## Where to put new variables

If a task needs a new or changed environment variable:

1. Add or update it in the matching `.env.example`.
2. Tell the user the exact key, suggested local value, and which `.env` they should update manually.

```text
# ❌ BAD — writing local secrets or defaults into .env
JWT_SECRET=dev-only-jwt-secret-change-before-any-real-use-32b

# ✅ GOOD — document the variable in .env.example
JWT_SECRET=dev-only-jwt-secret-change-before-any-real-use-32b
```

If `.env` is missing a required value, do not create or patch `.env`. Update `.env.example` and ask the user to copy the change themselves.
